though it was something of a box-office  disappointment when it first hit  theaters in 1997 looper sounds  whacked-out sci-fi epic The Fifth  Element has since become a cult classic  thanks to gonzo visuals and quirky  performances from stars Bruce Willis  Milla Jovovich and Chris Tucker  but despite the film's popularity  there's never been a follow-up here's a  look at some of the reasons why the  Fifth Element never got a sequel it  wasn't a huge hit The Fifth Element  didn't fare particularly well during its  theatrical release in the United States  which was a problem because at the time  the movies 90 million dollar plus price  tag made it the most expensive film ever  produced outside of Hollywood while the  film would go on to break some  international box office records by  pulling in two hundred and sixty three  million dollars worldwide it scored just  63 million dollars of that take from US  theaters which is probably a big reason  an equally expensive sequel wasn't quick  to get the green light the story was  complete vobis on originally envisioned  The Fifth Element as part of a trilogy  the story of main characters Corbin and  Lilu actually feels pretty complete at  the end of the film  he's rediscovered his vigor for life  she's found a reason to want to save the  earth the pair have found love together  and in the process they've literally  brought peace to the galaxy sure Besson  could probably find a clever way to  shake these two out of their well-earned  happiness at this point though it might  feel more like a desperate cash grab  especially considering Willis is now in  his mid 60's much as fans would like to  see more nobody wants to see the fifth  elements legacy tarnished by an  unnecessary second act  [Music]  very very sad off-screen drama  one of the biggest complicating factors  when it comes to a sequel is the  off-screen drama between Busan and yoga  vich during filming of The Fifth Element  the two became romantically involved and  got married not long after the film's  premiere it didn't last though as their  relationship fell apart on the set of  their 1999 collaboration the messenger  the story of Joan of Arc the film  flopped and the couple divorced the same  year still anything's possible  according to yoga vich their  relationship worked a lot better on set  than off set she told the Scottish Daily  Record if we could make movies to 365  days a year we would still be together  Luke and I have this unique creative  connection we did get married but we're  not together anymore in that sort of  particular context we're best friends  apart from being together or not he  loves me I love him for sans enigmatic  career when it comes to sequels you  should always strike while the iron's  hot but after the mess that was the  messenger the story of Joan of Arc and  the dissolution of his marriage to yoga  vich the Sun went the other direction  stepping away from directing entirely  for six years to focus on writing  instead that break may have squandered  any momentum for a Fifth Element sequel  but that's par for the course for Besson  who doesn't seem to be interested in  revisiting his past as a director other  than a couple of animated films beside  has never directed a sequel even passing  the reins of the proposed follow-up to  his 2014 hit Lucy it seems it would have  to take something pretty special to  convince him to return to the world of  the fifth element special effect  frustrations when the fifth element  arrived in theaters it was regarded as a  bona fide special-effects bonanza  but according to Masson the special  effects in the fifth element were  frustrating to work with because the  technology didn't exist yet to truly  fulfill his vision perhaps if the  effects had existed at the time the Sun  would have moved forward with the rest  of the trilogy but now that the  technology does finally exist he seems  to have moved on to other projects  speaking of which a spiritual sequel  dobersen has suggested in interviews  that he might be open to filming other  stories set in the same universe as the  fifth element for the last decade most  of his energy seems to have been turned  towards his big screen adaptation of the  long-running French comic book series  valerian and the city of a thousand  planets  filled with mind-blowing imagery only  made possible by advances in effects  over the past 20 years valerian and the  city of a thousand planets unfortunately  failed to launch at the box-office  taking in just two hundred and twenty  five million dollars worldwide against a  huge 177 million dollar budget since the  film underperformed the future of the  franchise is up in the air but  considering Besson was reportedly  planning to break his own rule against  sequels by turning Valyrian into a  trilogy it seems as though his heart  just isn't in the Fifth Element anymore  Never Say Never  though there's an argument to be made  that it's often better to leave your  fanbase wanting more but science still  shows a lot of affection for the world  of the fifth element and that would seem  to leave the door open if only slightly  for a sequel  even if Corbin and Lulu aren't at the  center of the story we'd still love to  see where the sign might take things  next just as long as it isn't a Ruby  rhod origin story all due respect to  Besson and Chris Tucker but some  characters are just more fun in small  doses thanks for watching click the  looper icon to subscribe to our YouTube  channel plus check out all these cool  stuff we know you love to  [Music]
 

